.TH CTHREADPOOL 7 "2026-10-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cthreadpool \- overview of the cthrpool thread pool and its futures
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A cthrpool runs tasks on a fixed number of worker threads, which it
starts when it is created and stops at its shutdown. A task is a
function plus an argument. Tasks wait in a queue that is either
unbounded or bounded, chosen by the queue_capacity of
cthrpool_create_mp(3): 0 gives an unbounded queue, and any other value a
bounded queue of that capacity.

A task can come with an on_complete callback, or it can return a
void * result through a future (cthrpool_submit_future(3)). A detached
future (cthrpool_future_create_detached(3)) has no pool behind it, and a
producer of your own completes it with cthrpool_future_fulfill(3).
.fi
.SS "Handles"
.nf
cthrpool is an opaque value handle, not a pointer, and CTHRPOOL_NULL
(0) means no pool. Every handle is checked before the pool is touched,
so a call through an invalid or destroyed handle reports
ccol_invalid_args, or does nothing for the calls that return no code.
cthrpool_destroy(3) of a stale handle, or a second destroy that races
the first one, stops the process.
.fi
.SS "Submitting a task"
.nf
There are four submit calls for each kind of task:
  - cthrpool_submit(3) and cthrpool_submit_future(3) wait for room in a
    full bounded queue;
  - cthrpool_try_submit(3) and cthrpool_try_submit_future(3) never wait,
    and give ccol_container_full instead;
  - cthrpool_timed_submit(3) and cthrpool_timed_submit_future(3) wait at
    most timeout_us microseconds, measured on CLOCK_MONOTONIC, and
    with a timeout of 0 they do exactly what the try_ call does.
A submit never waits on an unbounded queue. A submit after a shutdown
has started gives ccol_not_permitted. A blocking or timed submit into
a full bounded queue from a task of the same pool, or from its
on_complete, gives ccol_container_full at once, because only the
pool's workers can make room and they would be waiting for themselves.

Every future submit tells you why it failed: cthrpool_submit_future(3)
stores the code in its rv_out argument, and the try and timed forms
return it.
.fi
.SS "Completion callbacks"
.nf
on_complete runs exactly once for every task that a submit accepts.
A worker calls on_complete(arg, true) right after the task function
returns. A task that the pool discards before any worker takes it gets
on_complete(arg, false) instead, on the thread that discards it,
before that call returns and with no lock of the pool held, so
on_complete can always release what the task owns. A submit that fails
accepts nothing and never calls on_complete.
.fi
.SS "Futures and their results"
.nf
cthrpool_future_get(3) blocks until the task has a result and returns
it, or returns NULL for a task that a shutdown cancelled. Any number of
threads can wait on one future. The pool never allocates, copies or
frees a result: The thread that gets it owns it, and frees it the same
way the task allocated it. Call cthrpool_future_free(3) exactly once for
each future that a submit returned, whether you call
cthrpool_future_get(3) or not; without a get the future is
fire-and-forget. Futures use malloc(3) and free(3), never the pool's
allocator.
.fi
.SS "Shutdown and destroy"
.nf
cthrpool_shutdown_drain(3) finishes every queued task and then stops the
workers. cthrpool_shutdown_immediate(3) discards the queued tasks,
cancels their futures and calls their on_complete with ran == false,
lets each worker finish the task it is running, and then stops the
workers. An immediate shutdown that comes during a drain discards what
is left in the queue. Both calls block until every worker has exited,
and a second call of either one starts nothing new but waits for the
same end.

cthrpool_wait(3) blocks until the queue is empty and every running task
is done, without stopping the pool, and no other thread may submit
during that wait. cthrpool_destroy(3) runs a drain first if no shutdown
ran, waits for every call on the handle that is still running, frees
the pool and sets the handle to CTHRPOOL_NULL.
.fi
.SS "Calls from inside the pool"
.nf
A task, or an on_complete callback, can call into its own pool. A
cthrpool_wait(3), cthrpool_shutdown_drain(3) or cthrpool_shutdown_immediate(3)
from there returns at once and does nothing, because a worker cannot
wait for itself, and a cthrpool_destroy(3) of its own pool stops the
process. The same goes for a call inside an on_complete(arg, false)
that a shutdown runs.
.fi
.SS "fork(2)"
.nf
Create a pool after fork(), or call exec() right after fork(). A child
that keeps running without exec() while a pool from before the fork is
live is not supported, because fork() does not copy the worker
threads. See ccollections(7).
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Every function can be called from any thread at the same time as any
other one on the same live handle, shutdowns and a destroy included.
The one exception is a second cthrpool_destroy(3) of the same handle.
Tasks run on the worker threads, so data that two tasks share needs its
own protection.
.fi
